WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.179 --> 00:00:04.649
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.649 --> 00:00:09.640
This work is about adding color to black and
white images. There were some previous works

00:00:09.640 --> 00:00:14.280
that tackled this problem, and many of them
worked quite well, but there were cases when

00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:19.590
the results simply didn't make too much sense.
For instance, the algorithm often didn't guess

00:00:19.590 --> 00:00:22.570
what color the fur of a dog should be.

00:00:22.570 --> 00:00:27.710
If we would give the same task to a human,
we could usually expect better results because

00:00:27.710 --> 00:00:33.070
the human knows what breed the dog is, and
what colors are appropriate for that breed.

00:00:33.070 --> 00:00:38.040
In short, we know what is actually seen on
the image, but the algorithm doesn't - it

00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:43.860
just trains on black and white and colored
image pairs and learns how it is usually done

00:00:43.860 --> 00:00:48.960
without any concept of what is seen on the
image. So here is the idea - let's try to

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:54.219
get the neural network not only to colorize
the image, but classify what is seen on the

00:00:54.219 --> 00:00:59.340
image before doing that. If we see a dog in
an image, it is not that likely to be pink,

00:00:59.340 --> 00:01:03.870
is it? If we know that we have to deal with
a golf course, we immediately know to reach

00:01:03.870 --> 00:01:06.119
out for those green crayons.

00:01:06.119 --> 00:01:11.320
This is a novel fusion-based technique. This
means that we have a separate neural network

00:01:11.320 --> 00:01:17.439
for classifying the images and one for colorizing
them. The fusion part is when we unify the

00:01:17.439 --> 00:01:22.640
information in these neural networks so we
can create an output that aggregates all this

00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:28.710
information. And the results, are just spectacular,
the additional information on what these images

00:01:28.710 --> 00:01:32.780
are about really make a huge impact on the
quality of the results.

00:01:32.780 --> 00:01:37.600
Please note that this is by far not the first
work on fusion, I've also linked an earlier

00:01:37.600 --> 00:01:43.170
paper for recognizing objects in videos, but
I think this is a really creative application

00:01:43.170 --> 00:01:47.860
of the same train of thought that is really
worthy of our attention. To delight the fellow

00:01:47.860 --> 00:01:51.360
tinkerers out there, the source code of the
project is also available.

00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:57.250
The supplementary video reveals that temporal
coherence is still a problem. This means that

00:01:57.250 --> 00:02:02.930
every image is colorized separately with no
communication. It is a bit like giving the

00:02:02.930 --> 00:02:08.899
images to colorize one by one to different
people, with no overarching artistic direction.

00:02:08.899 --> 00:02:13.609
The result we'll get this way is a flickery
animation. This problem has been solved for

00:02:13.609 --> 00:02:17.980
artistic style transfer, which we have discussed
in an earlier episode, the link is in the

00:02:17.980 --> 00:02:19.469
description box.

00:02:19.469 --> 00:02:24.010
There was one future episode planned about
plastic deformations. I have read the paper

00:02:24.010 --> 00:02:29.370
several times, and it is excellent, but I
felt that the quality of my presentation was

00:02:29.370 --> 00:02:34.419
not up there to put it in front of you Fellow
Scholars. It may happen in the future, but

00:02:34.419 --> 00:02:39.659
I had to shelf this one for now. Please accept
my apologies for that. In the next episode,

00:02:39.659 --> 00:02:44.169
we'll continue with OpenAI's great new invention
for reinforcement learning.

00:02:44.169 --> 00:02:48.269
Thanks for watching, and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

